    "type": "python",
    "question": "Write `solve(data)` where `data` is a string. Return True if it is a palindrome (reads the same forwards and backwards) and False otherwise, ignoring case and any non-alphanumeric characters. You must scan with two pointers from both ends rather than building a reversed copy, so the check uses O(1) extra space.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: a string to test for palindrome (ignore case/punctuation)\n    # TODO: use two pointers moving inward\n    return False",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    left, right = 0, len(data) - 1                      # two pointers at the outer ends of the string\n    while left < right:                                  # converge until the pointers meet/cross\n        while left < right and not data[left].isalnum():  # skip non-alphanumeric on the left side\n            left += 1                                    # advance the left pointer past punctuation/spaces\n        while left < right and not data[right].isalnum():  # skip non-alphanumeric on the right side\n            right -= 1                                   # retreat the right pointer past punctuation/spaces\n        if data[left].lower() != data[right].lower():    # compare the two relevant chars case-insensitively\n            return False                                 # mismatch means it is not a palindrome\n        left += 1                                        # move inward from the left\n        right -= 1                                        # move inward from the right\n    return True                                           # all opposing chars matched -> palindrome",
