    "id": "py-c-21",
    "topicId": "python-topic-21",
    "category": "python",
    "title": "Stream Processing: Sliding-Window Maximum Throughput",
    "question": "You are processing a stream of metric events. `data` is a list `[events, window]` where `events` is a list of integers (one reading per tick, in arrival order) and `window` is a positive integer window size. For every position where a full window of `window` consecutive events is available, compute the maximum reading inside that window. Write `solve(data)` that returns the list of window maxima in order. NOTE: A real stream uses a bounded buffer; here we simulate the deterministic result over a finite list.",
    "type": "python",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    events, window = data[0], data[1]\n    # Return list of max value for each sliding window of size `window`\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    from collections import deque                       # deque gives O(1) pops from both ends\n    events, window = data[0], data[1]                    # unpack the stream and the window size\n    if window <= 0 or not events:                        # guard: no valid window or empty stream\n        return []                                        # nothing to emit\n    dq = deque()                                         # holds indices of useful candidates, values decreasing\n    result = []                                          # collected window maxima\n    for i, val in enumerate(events):                     # consume the stream tick by tick\n        while dq and events[dq[-1]] <= val:              # drop tail indices whose value can't beat current\n            dq.pop()                                     # they are dominated by the newer larger value\n        dq.append(i)                                     # current index is a fresh candidate\n        if dq[0] <= i - window:                          # front index has slid out of the window\n            dq.popleft()                                 # evict the stale maximum\n        if i >= window - 1:                              # a full window is now available\n            result.append(events[dq[0]])                 # front of deque is the current window max\n    return result                                        # emit the per-window maxima",
    "testCases": [
      {
        "input": "[[1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7], 3]",
        "expected": "[3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 7]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[4, 4, 4], 1]",
        "expected": "[4, 4, 4]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[], 3]",
        "expected": "[]"
      }
    ],
    "hint": "1. Keep a monotonic deque of indices whose values are strictly decreasing.\n2. The front always holds the index of the current window's maximum.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "exampleInput": "[[1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7], 3]",
    "exampleOutput": "[3, 3, 5, 5, 6, 7]",
    "constraints": "Up to 10^6 events. One pass expected; window fits in memory.",
    "hints": [
      "A monotonic deque amortizes each element being pushed and popped at most once.",
      "Evict the front index when it falls outside [i-window+1, i]."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "For each window, scan all `window` elements to find the max. Time Complexity: O(N*W).",
    "optimized": "Maintain a monotonic decreasing deque of indices. Time Complexity: O(N).",
