    "question": "Implement a record validator. `data` is a list of record dicts. A record is VALID only if: it has an integer `'id'` that is non-negative, a non-empty string `'name'`, and an `'email'` string containing exactly one '@'. Write `solve(data)` that returns a list `[valid_count, invalid_ids]` where `invalid_ids` is the list of `id` values (in input order) of records that FAIL any rule. If a failing record has no usable integer id, use the string `'?'` in its place.",
    "type": "python",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of record dicts {'id', 'name', 'email'}\n    # Return [valid_count, invalid_ids] (failed ids in input order; '?' if id unusable)\n    return [0, []]",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    valid = 0                                    # tally of records passing all rules\n    invalid_ids = []                             # ids of records that violate any rule\n    for rec in data:                             # validate each record independently\n        rid = rec.get('id')                      # fetch the id (may be missing/None)\n        ok = True                                # assume valid until a rule fails\n        if not isinstance(rid, int) or isinstance(rid, bool) or rid < 0:  # id must be a non-negative int (bool excluded)\n            ok = False                           # id rule failed\n        name = rec.get('name')                   # fetch the name field\n        if not isinstance(name, str) or name == '':  # name must be a non-empty string\n            ok = False                           # name rule failed\n        email = rec.get('email')                 # fetch the email field\n        if not isinstance(email, str) or email.count('@') != 1:  # email must have exactly one '@'\n            ok = False                           # email rule failed\n        if ok:                                   # all rules satisfied\n            valid += 1                           # count this record as valid\n        else:                                    # at least one rule failed\n            invalid_ids.append(rid if isinstance(rid, int) and not isinstance(rid, bool) else '?')  # record id or '?'\n    return [valid, invalid_ids]                  # summary: valid count and failing ids",
