    "id": "py-c-27",
    "topicId": "python-topic-27",
    "category": "python",
    "title": "Memory Optimization: Streaming Top-K Without Sorting All",
    "question": "You must find the K largest values from a huge stream without holding the full sorted dataset in memory. `data` is a list `[values, k]` where `values` is a list of integers and `k` is a positive integer. Write `solve(data)` that returns the K largest values in DESCENDING order. You may assume K is far smaller than N, so keep only a bounded heap of size K. If K >= N, return all values sorted descending.",
    "type": "python",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    values, k = data[0], data[1]\n    # Return the K largest values in descending order, using O(K) extra memory\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    import heapq                                 # heapq gives an O(log K) min-heap\n    values, k = data[0], data[1]                 # unpack the stream and K\n    if k <= 0:                                    # no elements requested\n        return []                                 # empty result\n    heap = []                                     # min-heap holding the K largest seen so far (O(K) memory)\n    for v in values:                             # consume the stream one value at a time\n        if len(heap) < k:                        # heap not yet full\n            heapq.heappush(heap, v)              # just add the value\n        elif v > heap[0]:                        # value beats the smallest of the current top-K\n            heapq.heapreplace(heap, v)           # pop smallest, push new value in one O(log K) op\n    return sorted(heap, reverse=True)            # return the K largest in descending order",
    "testCases": [
      {
        "input": "[[5, 1, 9, 3, 7, 2, 8], 3]",
        "expected": "[9, 8, 7]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[4, 4, 4, 1], 2]",
        "expected": "[4, 4]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[2, 1], 5]",
        "expected": "[2, 1]"
      }
    ],
    "hint": "1. Keep a size-K min-heap; the smallest of the top-K sits at the root.\n2. Replace the root only when a larger value arrives.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "exampleInput": "[[5, 1, 9, 3, 7, 2, 8], 3]",
    "exampleOutput": "[9, 8, 7]",
    "constraints": "N up to 10^8 streamed values; extra memory must be O(K), not O(N).",
    "hints": [
      "Sorting everything is O(N log N) time and O(N) memory; a heap is O(N log K) time, O(K) memory.",
      "heapreplace is cheaper than separate heappop + heappush."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "Sort the entire stream descending and take the first K. Time Complexity: O(N log N), O(N) memory.",
    "optimized": "Maintain a bounded size-K min-heap over the stream. Time Complexity: O(N log K), O(K) memory.",
    "complexity": {
      "time": "O(N log K)",
      "space": "O(K)"
    },
    "discussion": {
      "whyAsked": "Top-K over a stream tests the heap pattern and, crucially, bounding memory when data does not fit in RAM.",
      "mistakes": "Collecting everything before trimming (defeating the memory goal), or using a max-heap and never bounding it.",
      "followUps": [
        "How would you find the K smallest with the same memory budget?",
        "How does this generalize to a distributed top-K with partial merges?"
      ]
    }
