    "title": "Fibonacci Series: Nth Term, Iterative and Memory-Lean",
    "question": "Write `solve(data)` that takes a non-negative integer n and returns the n-th Fibonacci number, where F(0)=0, F(1)=1, F(n)=F(n-1)+F(n-2). Use constant extra memory (do not build the whole series list).",
    "type": "python",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: a non-negative integer n\n    # Return F(n) with F(0)=0, F(1)=1\n    return 0",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    n = data                                     # the index we want\n    if n == 0:                                   # base case F(0)\n        return 0                                 # F(0) = 0\n    prev, curr = 0, 1                            # rolling window: F(0) and F(1)\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):                    # iterate from F(2) up to F(n)\n        prev, curr = curr, prev + curr           # slide the window: new curr = sum of last two\n    return curr                                  # curr now holds F(n)",
