    "question": "The digital root of a number is obtained by repeatedly summing its digits until a single digit (0-9) remains. Write `solve(data)` that takes a non-negative integer and returns its digital root. Solve it without converting the number to a string (use arithmetic).",
    "type": "python",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: a non-negative integer\n    # Return its digital root (repeated digit sum down to one digit)\n    return 0",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    n = data                                     # the working number\n    while n >= 10:                               # keep going until a single digit remains\n        s = 0                                    # accumulator for this round's digit sum\n        while n > 0:                             # peel off digits with arithmetic (no str())\n            s += n % 10                          # add the least-significant digit\n            n //= 10                             # drop that digit\n        n = s                                    # replace n with the digit sum and repeat\n    return n                                     # single remaining digit is the digital root",
