    "id": "py-c-39",
    "topicId": "python-topic-39",
    "category": "python",
    "title": "Merge Intervals: Total Covered Length",
    "question": "Given a list of closed intervals `[start, end]`, compute the total length covered by their union (overlaps counted once). `data` is a list of `[start, end]` pairs with start <= end. Write `solve(data)` that returns the total covered length as an integer.",
    "type": "python",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of [start, end] intervals (start <= end)\n    # Return total length covered by the union of intervals\n    return 0",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    if not data:                                 # no intervals -> no coverage\n        return 0                                  # length is zero\n    intervals = sorted(data, key=lambda iv: iv[0])  # sort by start so overlaps are adjacent\n    total = 0                                     # accumulated covered length\n    cur_start, cur_end = intervals[0]             # current merged interval bounds\n    for s, e in intervals[1:]:                    # walk the remaining intervals\n        if s <= cur_end:                          # this interval overlaps/touches the current one\n            cur_end = max(cur_end, e)             # extend the current merged interval\n        else:                                     # disjoint interval starts\n            total += cur_end - cur_start          # bank the finished interval's length\n            cur_start, cur_end = s, e             # start a new current interval\n    total += cur_end - cur_start                  # add the final interval's length\n    return total                                  # total length of the union",
    "testCases": [
      {
        "input": "[[1, 3], [2, 5], [7, 9]]",
        "expected": "6"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[0, 10]]",
        "expected": "10"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]]",
        "expected": "3"
      }
    ],
    "hint": "1. Sort by start, then merge overlapping/touching intervals.\n2. Sum (end - start) over the merged intervals.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "exampleInput": "[[1, 3], [2, 5], [7, 9]]",
    "exampleOutput": "6",
    "constraints": "Up to 10^6 intervals. Touching intervals (s == cur_end) count as overlapping.",
    "hints": [
      "Sorting by start is what makes a single merge pass possible.",
      "Use s <= cur_end (not strict <) so touching intervals merge."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "Mark every covered integer point on a number line. Time Complexity: O(sum of lengths).",
    "optimized": "Sort by start and merge in one pass. Time Complexity: O(N log N).",
    "complexity": {
      "time": "O(N log N)",
      "space": "O(N)"
    },
    "discussion": {
      "whyAsked": "Interval merging is a core sweep-line skill used in scheduling, calendars, and range coverage.",
      "mistakes": "Forgetting to sort first, using strict < (splitting touching intervals), or banking the last interval.",
      "followUps": [
        "How would you instead return the merged interval list?",
        "How do you handle half-open intervals [start, end) for the same task?"
      ]
    }
