    "question": "The `events` table logs page views. We want, for each `page_id`, the total views and the date of its single most recent view. A naive approach uses correlated subqueries per row. Write an efficient query that produces `page_id`, `total_views`, and `last_view` using a single grouped scan.",
    "type": "sql",
    "setupSql": "CREATE TABLE events (id INT, page_id INT, view_date DATE);\nINSERT INTO events VALUES (1, 1, '2026-02-01'), (2, 1, '2026-02-03'), (3, 1, '2026-02-02'), (4, 2, '2026-02-05'), (5, 2, '2026-02-05'), (6, 3, '2026-02-01'), (7, 3, '2026-02-09'), (8, 3, '2026-02-04');",
    "initialCode": "-- Write your SQL query here\nSELECT * FROM events;",
    "solutionCode": "-- One pass over the table, grouped by page\nSELECT page_id,\n  -- total number of views for the page\n  COUNT(*) AS total_views,\n  -- the latest view date in the group\n  MAX(view_date) AS last_view\nFROM events\n-- aggregate per page in a single scan instead of per-row subqueries\nGROUP BY page_id\n-- deterministic ordering\nORDER BY page_id;",
