    "question": "The `events` table has duplicate rows per `user_id` (multiple updates). For each user, keep only the most recent row (largest `ts`); break ties on ts by larger `event_id`. Output `user_id`, `event_id`, `status`.",
    "type": "sql",
    "setupSql": "CREATE TABLE events (event_id INTEGER, user_id INTEGER, status TEXT, ts INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO events VALUES (1, 100, 'new', 10), (2, 100, 'active', 20), (3, 100, 'churned', 20), (4, 200, 'new', 5), (5, 300, 'active', 30), (6, 300, 'new', 15);",
    "initialCode": "-- Write your SQL query here\nSELECT * FROM events;",
    "solutionCode": "-- Number rows per user, newest first, deterministic tie-break\nWITH ranked AS (\n  SELECT event_id, user_id, status,\n    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY user_id\n      ORDER BY ts DESC, event_id DESC\n    ) AS rn\n  FROM events\n)\n-- Keep only the single newest row per user\nSELECT user_id, event_id, status\nFROM ranked\nWHERE rn = 1\n-- Deterministic ordering\nORDER BY user_id;",
