    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are reading a raw text file where each element of `data` is one CSV line of the form \"id,name,age\" (e.g. \"1,Alice,30\"). This mirrors spark.read loading raw text that you must parse and cast into a typed schema. Write `solve(data)` that returns a list of row-dicts {'id': int, 'name': str, 'age': int}, one per line, preserving file order. The inline comments show the equivalent PySpark read/cast API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of CSV strings like '1,Alice,30'\n    # Return list of {'id': int, 'name': str, 'age': int}\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df = spark.read.csv(path, schema='id INT, name STRING, age INT')  # read + apply schema\n    #   return [r.asDict() for r in df.collect()]                          # bring rows to driver\n    rows = []                                  # output buffer (one dict per parsed line)\n    for line in data:                          # scan each text line (Spark reads each record)\n        parts = line.split(\",\")               # split on comma (csv field delimiter)\n        rows.append({                           # build a typed Row from the string fields\n            \"id\": int(parts[0]),              # cast field 0 to INT (col('id').cast('int'))\n            \"name\": parts[1],                  # field 1 stays STRING\n            \"age\": int(parts[2]),             # cast field 2 to INT (col('age').cast('int'))\n        })\n    return rows                                 # collect() back to the driver as list of rows",
