    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given data = [rows, threshold] where rows is a list of {'dept': str, 'salary': int}. Emulate the Spark SQL query: SELECT dept, SUM(salary) AS total_salary FROM t GROUP BY dept HAVING SUM(salary) > threshold ORDER BY dept. Return a list of {'dept','total_salary'} for departments whose total strictly exceeds threshold, sorted by dept. Write `solve(data)`; comments show the equivalent spark.sql API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [rows, threshold]; rows are {'dept': str, 'salary': int}\n    # SELECT dept, SUM(salary) GROUP BY dept HAVING SUM(salary) > threshold ORDER BY dept\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df.createOrReplaceTempView('t')\n    #   spark.sql('''SELECT dept, SUM(salary) AS total_salary FROM t\n    #                GROUP BY dept HAVING SUM(salary) > {th} ORDER BY dept''')\n    rows = data[0]                             # the table rows (the temp view)\n    threshold = data[1]                        # the HAVING comparison value\n    sums = {}                                  # dept -> SUM(salary)\n    for r in rows:                             # scan the table (GROUP BY shuffles by dept)\n        sums[r[\"dept\"]] = sums.get(r[\"dept\"], 0) + r[\"salary\"]  # accumulate SUM(salary)\n    out = []                                   # rows surviving HAVING\n    for d in sorted(sums):                      # ORDER BY dept\n        if sums[d] > threshold:                 # HAVING SUM(salary) > threshold (post-aggregate)\n            out.append({\"dept\": d, \"total_salary\": sums[d]})\n    return out",
