    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given data = [rows, n] where rows is a list of {'key': str} and n is the number of partitions. Spark's repartition(n, 'key') sends each row to partition (hash(key) % n). Using a stable hash (sum of character codes) to be deterministic, return a list of length n giving the number of rows in each partition (index = partition id). Write `solve(data)`; comments show the equivalent PySpark repartition API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [rows, n]; rows are {'key': str}\n    # Return list of length n: count of rows in each partition (hash(key) % n)\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df.repartition(n, 'key')                                    # hash-partition by key\n    #     .rdd.glom().map(len).collect()                            # rows per partition\n    rows = data[0]                             # the rows to distribute\n    n = data[1]                                # target number of partitions\n    sizes = [0] * n                            # partition_id -> row count\n    for r in rows:                             # scan each row\n        p = (sum(ord(c) for c in r[\"key\"])) % n  # deterministic hash(key) % n (partitioner)\n        sizes[p] += 1                           # count one row into that partition\n    return sizes                                # glom().map(len): partition sizes",
