    "category": "pyspark",
    "title": "Broadcast-Enriching Facts With A Dimension",
    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given data = [facts, dim]. facts is a large list of {'id','code','value'} and dim is a small list of {'code','label'}. Broadcast the small dim and left-enrich each fact with its label; codes not in dim get label 'UNKNOWN'. Return rows {'id','label','value'} sorted by id. This mirrors facts.join(F.broadcast(dim), 'code', 'left') with a coalesce/fillna for misses. Write `solve(data)`; comments show the equivalent PySpark broadcast join API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [facts, dim]; facts {'id','code','value'}, dim {'code','label'}\n    # Left-enrich label from broadcast dim; misses -> 'UNKNOWN'; sort by id\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   facts.join(F.broadcast(dim), on='code', how='left')         # broadcast left join\n    #        .select('id', F.coalesce('label', F.lit('UNKNOWN')).alias('label'), 'value')\n    #        .orderBy('id')\n    facts = data[0]                            # the large fact side (stays partitioned)\n    dim = data[1]                              # the small dimension (broadcast to every executor)\n    lookup = {d[\"code\"]: d[\"label\"] for d in dim}  # the broadcast hash map\n    out = []                                   # enriched rows\n    for f in facts:                            # map-side join: probe broadcast hash, no shuffle\n        out.append({\n            \"id\": f[\"id\"],\n            \"label\": lookup.get(f[\"code\"], \"UNKNOWN\"),  # coalesce miss -> 'UNKNOWN'\n            \"value\": f[\"value\"],\n        })\n    out.sort(key=lambda x: x[\"id\"])          # orderBy('id') for deterministic output\n    return out",
