    "id": "pyspark-c-16",
    "topicId": "pyspark-topic-16",
    "category": "pyspark",
    "title": "Min-Max Normalizing A Value Column",
    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given rows: data = [{'id': int, 'v': number}, ...]. Min-max normalize the 'v' column to [0,1]: norm = round((v - min)/(max - min), 4); if all values are equal (range 0), norm is 0.0 for every row. Return rows {'id','norm'} sorted by id. This mirrors computing min/max with a window-less aggregate then a withColumn that references them (a large-scale map after a reduce). Write `solve(data)`; comments show the equivalent PySpark API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'id': int, 'v': number}\n    # Min-max normalize v to [0,1]; equal values -> 0.0; sort by id\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   stats = df.agg(F.min('v').alias('lo'), F.max('v').alias('hi')).first()  # reduce\n    #   df.withColumn('norm', F.round((F.col('v')-stats.lo)/(stats.hi-stats.lo),4))\n    #     .select('id','norm').orderBy('id')                                    # map\n    vals = [r[\"v\"] for r in data]              # extract the value column\n    if not vals:                                # empty DataFrame -> nothing to normalize\n        return []\n    lo, hi = min(vals), max(vals)              # F.min('v'), F.max('v') (the reduce stage)\n    rng = hi - lo                              # denominator of the normalization\n    out = []                                   # normalized rows (the map stage)\n    for r in data:                             # scan each row applying the formula\n        norm = 0.0 if rng == 0 else round((r[\"v\"] - lo) / rng, 4)  # guard divide-by-zero\n        out.append({\"id\": r[\"id\"], \"norm\": norm})\n    out.sort(key=lambda x: x[\"id\"])          # orderBy('id')\n    return out",
    "testCases": [
      {
        "input": "[{'id':1,'v':10},{'id':2,'v':20},{'id':3,'v':30}]",
        "expected": "[{'id': 1, 'norm': 0.0}, {'id': 2, 'norm': 0.5}, {'id': 3, 'norm': 1.0}]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[]",
        "expected": "[]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[{'id':1,'v':7},{'id':2,'v':7}]",
        "expected": "[{'id': 1, 'norm': 0.0}, {'id': 2, 'norm': 0.0}]"
      }
    ],
    "hint": "1. Compute min and max in one reduce pass.\n2. Map each row to (v-min)/(max-min), rounding to 4dp; guard the equal-values case.",
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "exampleInput": "[{'id':1,'v':10},{'id':2,'v':20},{'id':3,'v':30}]",
    "exampleOutput": "[{'id': 1, 'norm': 0.0}, {'id': 2, 'norm': 0.5}, {'id': 3, 'norm': 1.0}]",
    "constraints": "Up to 10^6 rows. If max==min, every norm is 0.0 (avoid divide-by-zero).",
    "hints": [
      "This is a reduce (min/max) followed by a broadcast-of-scalars map across all rows.",
      "Always guard the zero-range case before dividing."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "Recompute min and max inside the loop for every row. Time Complexity: O(N^2).",
    "optimized": "One pass for min/max, one pass to normalize. Time Complexity: O(N).",
    "complexity": {
      "time": "O(N)",
      "space": "O(N)"
    },
    "discussion": {
      "whyAsked": "Feature scaling is a standard ML-prep step; it tests combining an aggregate with a per-row map.",
      "mistakes": "Dividing by zero when all values match, or recomputing min/max per row.",
      "followUps": [
        "Why is broadcasting the scalar min/max cheaper than a self-join to attach them?",
        "How would you normalize per group instead of globally (window vs groupBy)?"
      ]
    }
