    "question": "You are given product rows: data = [{'sku': str, 'price': number or None, 'in_stock': bool}, ...]. Keep products that are in_stock AND priced below 100, while safely skipping rows whose price is None (a null never satisfies a comparison). Return the surviving SKUs sorted ascending. This mirrors df.filter(col('in_stock') & (col('price') < 100)) where null prices are excluded by SQL three-valued logic. Write `solve(data)`; comments show the equivalent PySpark filter API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'sku': str, 'price': number|None, 'in_stock': bool}\n    # Keep in_stock and price<100; null price -> excluded; return sorted SKUs\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df.filter(F.col('in_stock') & (F.col('price') < 100))   # null price -> NULL -> excluded\n    #     .select('sku').orderBy('sku')\n    out = []                                   # surviving SKUs\n    for r in data:                             # scan each row (filter is narrow)\n        price = r.get(\"price\")                 # may be None (a NULL in Spark)\n        if price is None:                       # three-valued logic: NULL < 100 is NULL, not True\n            continue                            # so null-priced rows never pass the filter\n        if r[\"in_stock\"] and price < 100:       # in_stock AND price < 100\n            out.append(r[\"sku\"])              # project the sku\n    return sorted(out)                          # orderBy('sku')",
