    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given person rows: data = [{'first': str, 'last': str, 'age': int}, ...]. Select a new shape: a 'full_name' column built as first + ' ' + last, and the existing 'age' column. Write `solve(data)` returning a list of dicts {'full_name': str, 'age': int} preserving input order. NOTE: This mirrors df.select(F.concat_ws(' ', 'first', 'last').alias('full_name'), 'age'). Because a real Spark session cannot run in the browser, implement the logic in pure Python; the inline comments show the equivalent PySpark DataFrame API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'first': str, 'last': str, 'age': int}\n    # Return [{'full_name': first+' '+last, 'age': age}] preserving order.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df = spark.createDataFrame(data)\n    #   out = df.select(F.concat_ws(\" \", \"first\", \"last\").alias(\"full_name\"),  # build + alias new column\n    #                   \"age\")                                                   # keep age\n    #   return [r.asDict() for r in out.collect()]\n    result = []                                        # collect buffer\n    for row in data:                                   # scan each partition row (narrow select)\n        full_name = row[\"first\"] + \" \" + row[\"last\"]  # concat_ws(' ', first, last)\n        result.append({                                # project the new shape\n            \"full_name\": full_name,                    # computed/aliased column\n            \"age\": row[\"age\"],                         # passthrough column\n        })\n    return result                                      # collect() to driver",
