    "id": "pyspark-c-42",
    "topicId": "pyspark-topic-42",
    "category": "pyspark",
    "title": "UDF vs Built-in: Normalize Phone Numbers",
    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given data = [str, ...] of raw phone numbers. Normalize each: strip every non-digit character, then keep only the last 10 digits (drop a country prefix). If fewer than 10 digits remain, return None for that entry. Write `solve(data)` returning a list of normalized strings (or None). NOTE: This mirrors applying a PySpark UDF (or regexp_replace) over a column. A real Spark session cannot run here; implement in pure Python; inline comments show the equivalent API and why a built-in is preferred over a Python UDF.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of raw phone-number strings\n    # Keep digits only, take last 10; return None if <10 digits.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent (prefer a BUILT-IN over a Python UDF to avoid per-row serialization):\n    #   digits = F.regexp_replace('phone', '[^0-9]', '')          # vectorized, runs in the JVM\n    #   norm = F.when(F.length(digits) >= 10, F.expr('substring(digits, length(digits)-9, 10)'))\n    #   df.withColumn('norm', norm)\n    # A Python UDF would be: udf(normalize, StringType()) but it serializes each row to Python (slow).\n    def normalize(s):                            # the per-row function the UDF would wrap\n        digits = ''.join(ch for ch in s if ch.isdigit())  # regexp_replace('[^0-9]','')\n        if len(digits) < 10:                     # guard: not enough digits\n            return None                          # produce a null (F.when ... otherwise null)\n        return digits[-10:]                      # take the last 10 digits (drop country code)\n    return [normalize(s) for s in data]          # map the function across the column",
    "testCases": [
      {
        "input": "['+1 (415) 555-2671','044-9988-7766-55','123']",
        "expected": "['4155552671', '9988776655', None]"
      },
      {
        "input": "['18005551234']",
        "expected": "['8005551234']"
      },
      {
        "input": "[]",
        "expected": "[]"
      }
    ],
    "hint": "1. Filter to digits, then slice the last 10.\n2. Prefer regexp_replace over a Python UDF for speed.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "exampleInput": "['+1 (415) 555-2671','044-9988-7766-55','123']",
    "exampleOutput": "['4155552671', '9988776655', None]",
    "constraints": "Up to 10^6 rows. Strings may contain spaces, +, -, parentheses.",
    "hints": [
      "Python UDFs serialize each row between the JVM and Python, killing throughput.",
      "Built-in column expressions (regexp_replace, substring) stay in the JVM and vectorize."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "A Python UDF applied per row with full string parsing. Time Complexity: O(N*L) plus serialization overhead.",
    "optimized": "Same O(N*L) logic but via built-in column functions with no Python round-trip. Time Complexity: O(N*L).",
