    "question": "You are given data = {'rows': [{'id': int, 'amount': float}, ...], 'threshold': float}. Emulate the Spark SQL query: SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders WHERE amount > threshold. Write `solve(data)` that returns the integer count. NOTE: This mirrors spark.sql(...) over a temp view. A real Spark session cannot run here; implement in pure Python; inline comments show the equivalent SQL/DataFrame API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: {'rows': [{'id','amount'}], 'threshold': float}\n    # Return COUNT(*) WHERE amount > threshold.\n    return 0",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df.createOrReplaceTempView('orders')\n    #   spark.sql('SELECT COUNT(*) AS c FROM orders WHERE amount > {t}'.format(t=data['threshold']))\n    #   return result.collect()[0]['c']\n    t = data['threshold']                        # the WHERE-clause comparison value\n    count = 0                                     # running count (the COUNT(*) accumulator)\n    for row in data['rows']:                      # scan the temp view's rows once\n        if row['amount'] > t:                     # apply the WHERE predicate (filter pushdown)\n            count += 1                            # row qualifies -> increment COUNT(*)\n    return count                                  # single scalar back to the driver",
