    "type": "pyspark",
    "question": "You are given rows as a list of dicts: data = [{'id': int, 'tags': [str, ...]}, ...]. Flatten (explode) each row so every tag becomes its own row. Drop rows whose tag list is empty (explode produces no rows for empty arrays). Write `solve(data)` that returns a list of dicts {'id', 'tag'} preserving input order then tag order. NOTE: This mirrors a large-scale transformation using F.explode. A real Spark session cannot run here, so implement the logic in pure Python; the inline comments show the equivalent PySpark DataFrame API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'id': int, 'tags': [str,...]}\n    # Explode tags into one row per tag; drop rows with empty tags. Return [{'id','tag'}].\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df = df.withColumn('tag', F.explode(F.col('tags'))).select('id','tag')\n    #   return [r.asDict() for r in df.collect()]\n    out = []                                      # exploded result rows\n    for row in data:                              # scan each parent row\n        for tag in row[\"tags\"]:                  # explode: emit one output row per array element\n            out.append({\"id\": row[\"id\"], \"tag\": tag})  # F.explode drops empties automatically\n    return out                                     # order = input order, then tag order",
