    "question": "You are given rows as a list of dicts: data = [{'first': str, 'last': str, 'price': int, 'qty': int}, ...]. Produce a projection that: renames 'first'->'fname', concatenates first and last with a space into 'full_name', and computes 'total' = price * qty. The output of each row must have exactly the keys: fname, full_name, total. Write `solve(data)` that returns the list of projected dicts in input order. NOTE: This mirrors df.select with alias, concat and arithmetic expressions. A real Spark session cannot run here, so implement the logic in pure Python; the inline comments show the equivalent PySpark DataFrame API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'first','last','price','qty'}\n    # Project to {'fname','full_name','total'}: rename first, concat names, price*qty. Preserve order.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   df = df.select(F.col('first').alias('fname'),\n    #                  F.concat_ws(' ', F.col('first'), F.col('last')).alias('full_name'),\n    #                  (F.col('price') * F.col('qty')).alias('total'))\n    #   return [r.asDict() for r in df.collect()]\n    out = []                                      # projected rows\n    for r in data:                                # scan each row (select is a narrow transformation)\n        out.append({\n            \"fname\": r[\"first\"],                # F.col('first').alias('fname')\n            \"full_name\": r[\"first\"] + \" \" + r[\"last\"],  # F.concat_ws(' ', first, last)\n            \"total\": r[\"price\"] * r[\"qty\"],     # (price * qty).alias('total')\n        })\n    return out                                     # collect() preserving input order",
