    "question": "You are given data = {'left': [{'id': int, 'name': str}, ...], 'right': [{'id': int, 'dept': str}, ...]}. Perform a LEFT OUTER join on id: every left row is kept; if there is no matching right row, set dept to 'UNKNOWN'. Assume id is unique in right. Write `solve(data)` that returns a list of dicts {'id', 'name', 'dept'} in left-row order. NOTE: This mirrors left.join(right, 'id', 'left').fillna('UNKNOWN'). A real Spark session cannot run here, so implement the logic in pure Python; the inline comments show the equivalent PySpark DataFrame API.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: {'left': [{'id','name'},...], 'right': [{'id','dept'},...]}\n    # Left outer join on id; fill missing dept with 'UNKNOWN'. Keep left order.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # PySpark equivalent:\n    #   joined = left.join(right, on='id', how='left').fillna({'dept': 'UNKNOWN'})\n    #   return [r.asDict() for r in joined.select('id','name','dept').collect()]\n    right_map = {}                                # build a lookup from the right side keyed by id\n    for r in data[\"right\"]:                       # scan right rows once\n        right_map[r[\"id\"]] = r[\"dept\"]           # id -> dept (id unique in right)\n    out = []                                       # joined rows, left order preserved\n    for l in data[\"left\"]:                         # iterate the LEFT side (all left rows survive)\n        dept = right_map.get(l[\"id\"], \"UNKNOWN\")  # matched dept, else null filled with 'UNKNOWN'\n        out.append({\"id\": l[\"id\"], \"name\": l[\"name\"], \"dept\": dept})  # emit joined row\n    return out                                     # collect() in left order",
