    "type": "data-engineering",
    "question": "Architecture Design (sharding): you are given input = [keys, num_partitions] where keys is a list of strings and num_partitions is an int. Assign each key to a partition using a deterministic hash: partition = (sum of ASCII codes of the key's characters) % num_partitions. Write `solve(data)` that returns a dict {partition_index: [keys...]} where each partition's key list is sorted ascending. Omit partitions that receive no keys.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [keys, num_partitions]\n    # partition = (sum of ord(c) for c in key) % num_partitions\n    # Return {partition_index: sorted([keys])}, omitting empty partitions.\n    return {}",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    keys, num_partitions = data[0], data[1]      # unpack key list and the partition count\n    partitions = {}                              # partition_index -> list of keys routed there\n    for key in keys:                             # route every key independently\n        h = sum(ord(c) for c in key)            # deterministic hash: sum of char codes\n        p = h % num_partitions                  # map the hash onto a partition slot\n        partitions.setdefault(p, []).append(key)  # append key to its partition's bucket\n    # sort each partition's keys for deterministic, comparable output\n    return {p: sorted(ks) for p, ks in partitions.items()}",
