    "title": "Clean, Join and Aggregate Orders",
    "type": "data-engineering",
    "question": "ETL Design (clean + join + aggregate): you are given input = [orders, customers] where orders is a list of dicts {'order_id': int, 'customer_id': int, 'amount': float} and customers is a list of dicts {'customer_id': int, 'name': str}. Clean orders by dropping rows whose amount is None or negative, join each remaining order to its customer name (drop orders whose customer_id has no match), then aggregate total spend per customer name. Write `solve(data)` that returns a list of {'name': str, 'total': float} sorted by name ascending.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [orders, customers]\n    # orders: {'order_id','customer_id','amount'}; customers: {'customer_id','name'}\n    # Clean (amount not None and >= 0), inner-join on customer_id, sum amount per name.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    orders, customers = data[0], data[1]         # unpack the two source tables\n    name_by_id = {c[\"customer_id\"]: c[\"name\"] for c in customers}  # build the customer lookup (broadcast dim)\n    totals = {}                                  # name -> running total spend\n    for o in orders:                             # scan the fact (orders) table\n        amt = o[\"amount\"]                       # the measure we aggregate\n        if amt is None or amt < 0:              # data-quality filter: drop null/negative amounts\n            continue                            # skip dirty row\n        cid = o[\"customer_id\"]                  # join key\n        if cid not in name_by_id:               # inner join: drop orders with no matching customer\n            continue                            # skip unmatched row\n        name = name_by_id[cid]                  # resolved dimension attribute\n        totals[name] = totals.get(name, 0.0) + amt  # accumulate spend per customer name\n    # emit sorted by name for deterministic output\n    return [{\"name\": n, \"total\": totals[n]} for n in sorted(totals)]",
