    "title": "Latest-Wins Merge with LSN Tiebreak",
    "type": "data-engineering",
    "question": "CDC Ingestion with out-of-order events: you receive change events that may arrive out of order, each a dict {'id': int, 'lsn': int, 'value': str} where lsn is a monotonically increasing log sequence number. For each id keep only the event with the largest lsn (the most recent change), regardless of arrival order. Write `solve(data)` that returns the merged current state as a list of {'id': int, 'value': str} sorted by id ascending.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'id': int, 'lsn': int, 'value': str} (possibly out of order)\n    # Keep the max-lsn event per id; return {'id','value'} sorted by id.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    latest = {}                                  # id -> (best_lsn, value) seen so far\n    for ev in data:                              # scan the (out-of-order) change stream once\n        rid = ev[\"id\"]                          # record primary key\n        lsn = ev[\"lsn\"]                         # log sequence number = recency ordering\n        # adopt this event only if it is strictly newer than the stored one\n        if rid not in latest or lsn > latest[rid][0]:\n            latest[rid] = (lsn, ev[\"value\"])    # newest LSN wins, store its value\n    # materialize current state sorted by id for deterministic output\n    return [{\"id\": rid, \"value\": latest[rid][1]} for rid in sorted(latest)]",
