    "question": "Data Modeling (collapse a snowflake into a star): you are given input = [products, categories] where products is a list of dicts {'product_id': int, 'name': str, 'category_id': int} and categories is a list of dicts {'category_id': int, 'category_name': str}. Denormalize by folding the category name directly into each product row. Write `solve(data)` that returns a list of {'product_id': int, 'name': str, 'category_name': str} sorted by product_id ascending. If a product's category_id is missing from categories, use 'Unknown'.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [products, categories]\n    # products: {'product_id','name','category_id'}; categories: {'category_id','category_name'}\n    # Denormalize category_name into each product; missing -> 'Unknown'. Sort by product_id.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    products, categories = data[0], data[1]      # unpack the two snowflake tables\n    cat_name = {c[\"category_id\"]: c[\"category_name\"] for c in categories}  # category lookup\n    out = []                                     # denormalized product rows\n    for p in products:                           # scan every product\n        name = cat_name.get(p[\"category_id\"], \"Unknown\")  # fold in category name, default 'Unknown'\n        out.append({\"product_id\": p[\"product_id\"], \"name\": p[\"name\"], \"category_name\": name})  # flat row\n    # sort by product_id for deterministic output\n    return sorted(out, key=lambda r: r[\"product_id\"])",
