    "id": "de-c-17",
    "topicId": "data-engineering-topic-17",
    "category": "data-engineering",
    "title": "Round-Robin Task Partition Balancer",
    "type": "data-engineering",
    "question": "Architecture Design (load balancing): you are given input = [tasks, num_workers] where tasks is a list of dicts {'task_id': int, 'cost': int} and num_workers is an int. Assign tasks to workers using a greedy least-loaded strategy: process tasks in input order and assign each to the worker with the smallest current total cost, breaking ties by the smallest worker index. Write `solve(data)` that returns a list of total costs per worker, indexed 0..num_workers-1.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: [tasks, num_workers]; tasks = list of {'task_id': int, 'cost': int}\n    # Greedily assign each task (in order) to the least-loaded worker (tie -> smallest index).\n    # Return list of total cost per worker, index 0..num_workers-1.\n    return []",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    tasks, num_workers = data[0], data[1]        # unpack task list and worker count\n    loads = [0] * num_workers                    # running total cost per worker, all start empty\n    for t in tasks:                              # assign tasks in arrival order\n        cost = t[\"cost\"]                        # this task's weight\n        # find the least-loaded worker; ties resolved by smallest index (min returns first on ties)\n        best = min(range(num_workers), key=lambda w: loads[w])\n        loads[best] += cost                     # place the task on that worker\n    return loads                                 # final cost per worker by index",
    "testCases": [
      {
        "input": "[[{'task_id':1,'cost':5},{'task_id':2,'cost':3},{'task_id':3,'cost':4}], 2]",
        "expected": "[5, 7]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[], 3]",
        "expected": "[0, 0, 0]"
      },
      {
        "input": "[[{'task_id':1,'cost':2},{'task_id':2,'cost':2},{'task_id':3,'cost':2}], 2]",
        "expected": "[4, 2]"
      }
    ],
    "hint": "1. Keep a per-worker running load list.\n2. For each task pick the min-load worker (ties -> smallest index) and add the cost.",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "exampleInput": "[[{'task_id':1,'cost':5},{'task_id':2,'cost':3},{'task_id':3,'cost':4}], 2]",
    "exampleOutput": "[5, 7]",
    "constraints": "Up to 10^5 tasks, num_workers >= 1. Greedy least-loaded; ties go to the smallest worker index.",
    "hints": [
      "Greedy least-loaded assignment approximates balanced partitioning of skewed task costs.",
      "Python's min over indices returns the first (smallest-index) worker on a tie automatically."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "Linear-scan all workers per task to find the minimum load. Time Complexity: O(N*W).",
    "optimized": "A min-heap of (load, index) gives O(log W) per task. Time Complexity: O(N log W).",
