    "type": "data-engineering",
    "question": "An ETL job must enrich and aggregate fact rows. You are given data = {'orders': [{'order_id': int, 'cust_id': int, 'amount': float}, ...], 'customers': [{'cust_id': int, 'segment': str}, ...]}. Join each order to its customer's segment (inner join on cust_id), then compute total amount per segment. Orders whose cust_id has no matching customer are dropped. Write `solve(data)` that returns a dict mapping segment -> total amount.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: {'orders': [...], 'customers': [...]}\n    # Inner-join orders to customers on cust_id, sum amount per segment.\n    return {}",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    seg_of = {}                                       # build a lookup: cust_id -> segment (the dimension table)\n    for c in data[\"customers\"]:                       # scan the small dimension table once\n        seg_of[c[\"cust_id\"]] = c[\"segment\"]          # index it by join key for O(1) probing (hash/broadcast join)\n    totals = {}                                        # accumulator: segment -> running revenue\n    for o in data[\"orders\"]:                          # stream the large fact table once\n        cid = o[\"cust_id\"]                            # the foreign key used for the join\n        if cid in seg_of:                             # inner join: skip orphan orders with no customer\n            seg = seg_of[cid]                         # resolved segment for this order\n            totals[seg] = totals.get(seg, 0) + o[\"amount\"]  # add into the per-segment bucket (groupBy sum)\n    return totals                                      # collect the aggregated result",
