    "title": "Star Schema Surrogate Key Assignment",
    "type": "data-engineering",
    "question": "When loading a dimension table you must assign stable integer surrogate keys to natural business keys. Given data = ['SKU-9', 'SKU-1', 'SKU-9', 'SKU-3', 'SKU-1'] (a list of natural keys in arrival order), assign surrogate keys starting at 1 in first-seen order. Write `solve(data)` that returns a dict mapping each natural key -> its surrogate key.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of natural-key strings in arrival order\n    # Assign surrogate ints starting at 1 in first-seen order.\n    return {}",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    keys = {}                                  # natural key -> surrogate key (the dimension's key map)\n    next_id = 1                                # surrogate keys are a monotonically increasing counter\n    for nk in data:                            # process natural keys in arrival order\n        if nk not in keys:                     # only the first sighting mints a new surrogate (idempotent)\n            keys[nk] = next_id                 # assign the current counter value as the surrogate\n            next_id += 1                       # advance so the next new key gets a fresh id\n    return keys                                # the natural->surrogate mapping for the dimension",
