    "question": "Implement log compaction over a CDC change log. You receive data, an ordered list of {'seq': int, 'id': int, 'op': 'U'|'D', 'value': int}. Like Kafka log compaction, only the most recent op per id matters: if the last op for an id is 'U', keep its value; if it is 'D', the key is removed entirely. Write solve(data) that returns the compacted state as [{'id': int, 'value': int}] sorted by id ascending.",
    "initialCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'seq': int, 'id': int, 'op': 'U'|'D', 'value': int} in seq order.\n    # Compact the log: keep only the LAST op per id. If the last op is 'D', the key is removed.\n    # Return surviving rows as [{'id','value'}] sorted by id.\n    return []\n",
    "solutionCode": "def solve(data):\n    # data: list of {'seq': int, 'id': int, 'op': 'U'|'D', 'value': int} ordered by seq\n    # Log compaction (Kafka-style): keep only the LAST op per id; a final 'D' removes the key.\n    last = {}                                      # id -> the most recent change event for it\n    for ev in data:                                # replay the log in order\n        last[ev['id']] = ev                        # overwrite: only the newest op per key survives\n    out = []                                       # compacted log: the net effect per id\n    for rid in sorted(last):                       # deterministic order by id\n        ev = last[rid]                             # the surviving (latest) event for this id\n        if ev['op'] == 'D':                        # if the net effect is a delete\n            continue                               # the key is gone; drop it from the compacted state\n        out.append({'id': rid, 'value': ev['value']})  # otherwise keep its final value\n    return out                                     # compacted current state\n",
